an the 74 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (40). Salem's all-time high is 117\u00b0F, on June 28, 2021.","t_chg":"Salem's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days warmed over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 127 complete years: Salem's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.16) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Salem's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.23), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1893. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.78\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +1.03) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in 7 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Salem's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.08 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 40.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1996 (66.96 in); the driest, 2013 (23.59 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}